However, there are some instances where the actions taken did not produce the expected effect. For example, when the cart position was near -20 and the pole angle was around -5, moving left (action 1) did not seem to help in balancing the pole. In such cases, moving right (action 2) might have been a better choice.

One observation that stands out is that when the cart velocity was high (either positive or negative), my current strategy tended to move the cart in the same direction as the velocity. This might not be optimal, as it could lead to increased oscillations and make it harder to balance the pole.

Based on this analysis, I propose updating the rules for keeping the pole balanced as follows:

1. IF Pole Angle > 10 OR Angular Velocity > 1 THEN move right (2)
   ELSE IF Pole Angle < -10 OR Angular Velocity < -1 THEN move left (1)

2. IF -10 <= Pole Angle <= 10 AND -1 <= Angular Velocity <= 1 THEN 
